{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "אותם", :translit "otam", :gloss "them"} {:he "זה הנפש", :translit "zeh ha-nefesh", :gloss "this is the soul"} {:he "אותם מלא וי\"ו", :translit "otam malei vav", :gloss "otam plene with vav"} {:he "אש זרה", :translit "esh zarah", :gloss "strange fire"} {:he "הורו בפני רבם", :translit "horu bifnei rabam", :gloss "they ruled in the presence of their master"} {:he "שתויי יין", :translit "shetuyei yayin", :gloss "intoxicated with wine"} {:he "בנים לא היו להם", :translit "banim lo hayu lahem", :gloss "they had no children"} {:he "נתנו עיניהם בפרנסות", :translit "natnu eineihem be-parnasot", :gloss "they set their eyes on sustenance"} {:he "לא נטלו עצה", :translit "lo natlu etzah", :gloss "they did not take counsel"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "אותם", :b "זה הנפש"} {:kind :other, :note "Plene spelling אותם with vav yields six total letters, matching the six sins enumerated."}], :frame {:text "The deaths of Nadav and Avihu are attributed to multiple sins in rabbinic sources. Leviticus 10:1 states they offered strange fire; the same chapter records that Aaron was commanded not to drink wine when entering the Tent, from which it is derived they were intoxicated with wine (Leviticus 10:9). It is said they died childless, as implied by Leviticus 10:14 directing Moses to speak to Aaron's remaining sons about the priesthood; and the verse states they died 'before Hashem,' which is read as dying in the presence of their master Moses (Leviticus 10:2). The Talmud reports the tradition that Nadav and Avihu refrained from marrying, deeming no woman worthy of their elevated status, and that they issued a halakhic ruling in Moses's presence (Sanhedrin 52a; Eruvin 63a).", :sources ["Leviticus 10:1" "Leviticus 10:2" "Leviticus 10:9" "Leviticus 10:14" "Sanhedrin 52a" "Eruvin 63a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "אותם", :a-value 447, :b "זה הנפש", :b-value 447, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :other, :no